{
  "id": "r-08",
  "level": 2,
  "title": "filter: picking rows",
  "explanation": "**filter()** keeps only the rows that match a condition, like a filter in Excel. You write the condition with comparisons: `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==` (equal) and `!=` (not equal).",
  "datasets": [
    "players"
  ],
  "starter_code": "",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "prompt": "Keep the players with more than 200 hours played. Store the result in result.",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "player_id",
          "username",
          "country",
          "signup_date",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            1,
            "ShadowFox",
            "USA",
            "2021-03-14",
            320
          ],
          [
            3,
            "NoScopeNate",
            "USA",
            "2020-11-23",
            610
          ],
          [
            5,
            "BlockBuilder",
            "UK",
            "2021-08-30",
            275
          ],
          [
            10,
            "xXDragonXx",
            "USA",
            "2021-03-01",
            502
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "filter"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "The condition goes inside filter().",
        "result <- players |> filter(hours_played > 200)"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> filter(hours_played > 200)"
    },
    {
      "teach": "`==` compares, while `=` is for naming things inside functions. A filter with one `=` gives an error that suggests `==`.",
      "prompt": "Keep only the players from Japan. Text goes in quotes, and \"equal\" is two equals signs.",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "player_id",
          "username",
          "country",
          "signup_date",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            4,
            "luna_plays",
            "Japan",
            "2023-01-09",
            88
          ],
          [
            8,
            "mochi",
            "Japan",
            "2024-02-29",
            12
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "filter"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "Use == and put Japan in quotes.",
        "result <- players |> filter(country == \"Japan\")"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> filter(country == \"Japan\")"
    },
    {
      "teach": "A **comma** between conditions means AND. You can also write `&`. For OR, use `|`.",
      "prompt": "Keep players from the USA with fewer than 400 hours. Separate two conditions with a comma: both must be true.",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "player_id",
          "username",
          "country",
          "signup_date",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            1,
            "ShadowFox",
            "USA",
            "2021-03-14",
            320
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "filter"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "filter(condition1, condition2) keeps rows where both are TRUE.",
        "result <- players |> filter(country == \"USA\", hours_played < 400)"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> filter(country == \"USA\", hours_played < 400)"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Keep players from Japan OR Brazil, using |.",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "player_id",
          "username",
          "country",
          "signup_date",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            4,
            "luna_plays",
            "Japan",
            "2023-01-09",
            88
          ],
          [
            6,
            "speedy_g",
            "Brazil",
            "2024-05-17",
            40
          ],
          [
            8,
            "mochi",
            "Japan",
            "2024-02-29",
            12
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "filter"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "| means OR.",
        "result <- players |> filter(country == \"Japan\" | country == \"Brazil\")"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> filter(country == \"Japan\" | country == \"Brazil\")"
    },
    {
      "teach": "**%in%** asks \"is this value in the list?\". It's a tidy replacement for lots of `|`s.",
      "prompt": "Last one: the same, but shorter, with %in%: keep players whose country is in c(\"Japan\", \"Mexico\", \"Philippines\").",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "player_id",
          "username",
          "country",
          "signup_date",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            4,
            "luna_plays",
            "Japan",
            "2023-01-09",
            88
          ],
          [
            7,
            "CaptainCrit",
            "Philippines",
            "2022-12-05",
            198
          ],
          [
            8,
            "mochi",
            "Japan",
            "2024-02-29",
            12
          ],
          [
            9,
            "TurboTaco",
            "Mexico",
            "2023-06-21",
            133
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "filter",
          "%in%"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "%in% checks against a vector of values.",
        "result <- players |> filter(country %in% c(\"Japan\", \"Mexico\", \"Philippines\"))"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> filter(country %in% c(\"Japan\", \"Mexico\", \"Philippines\"))"
    }
  ],
  "recap": "filter() keeps rows that match a condition. == compares, commas (or &) mean AND, | means OR, and %in% matches any value in a list."
}